WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.450
There's plenty of callbacks to The Shining
in Mike Flanagan's Doctor Sleep.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.469
There are also references to Stephen King's
Dark Tower series, cameos from familiar faces,

00:00:09.469 --> 00:00:11.420
and plenty more things to discover.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:16.060
No need to ask Tony for help; here are all
the Easter eggs you missed in Doctor Sleep.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.839
Much like Dan Torrance is haunted by what
he saw as a child in the Overlook Hotel, so

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.070
too is Doctor Sleep haunted by the visuals
of The Shining.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:28.640
For starters, the film pulls direct footage
from the 1980 horror masterpiece throughout

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.900
the runtime as Dan remembers his experiences
as a child.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.660
Slightly less obvious are the visual homages,
which include a nigh-identical tracking shot

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.680
as Dan and Abra drive up to the Overlook Hotel
just like his father did 40 years ago.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.480
Dan follows in his father's footsteps in other
ways, too - while meeting with Dr. John after

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.480
landing in Frazier, New Hampshire, Dan is
offered a job.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:54.060
In the scene, Dr. John's office looks nearly
identical to the office of the manager in

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.320
The Shining, which saw Jack Torrance get the
job as the caretaker for the Overlook.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:04.460
One final reference is Abra's home address:
1-9-8-0, or 1980, the year that The Shining

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:05.540
was originally released.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.400
Although Jack and Wendy don't make much more
than cameos in Doctor Sleep, their presence

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.260
haunts Dan.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.960
We find out that his resolution to not use
his Shining comes from trauma he experienced

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.300
at seeing his mother's death approach, while
his alcoholism is an unwelcome present from

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.000
his father.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.460
His parents haunt the film visually, as well.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.760
While trying to keep Abra away from Rose the
Hat once the pair return to the Overlook for

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.420
the final battle, Dan is injured by Rose and
possessed by the spirits of the hotel.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.800
When he meets Abra again, he's limping and
carrying an axe the same way his father chased

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.560
him through the hotel years ago.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.740
When he tries to keep Rose the Hat from approaching
him on the steps, it's a mirror performance

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.500
of the way Wendy Torrance keeps Jack away
with wild swings of a baseball bat in Kubrick's

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.000
film.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:52.760
While Doctor Sleep is definitely a sequel
to Kubrick's version of The Shining, it's

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.240
also a sequel to King's original story.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.460
"What about room 237?"

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.200
"Room 237?"

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.220
In the book, the forbidden room in the Overlook
is 217, rather than 237.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.320
Kubrick changed the numbers at the hotel owners'
request.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.760
The owners feared that if the director used
an actual room number at the Timberline Hotel,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.790
where The Shining was filmed, no one would
want to book the room.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:23.409
Doctor Sleep uses the more well-known 237
number, but nods to the original book by having

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:26.900
Dan step through room 217 at the hospice where
he works.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.280
Some other major references to the original
novel come when Dan and Abra return to the

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:36.170
Overlook; while there, Dan runs into Lloyd
the bartender, who may or may not be the ghost

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.160
of Jack Torrance.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:42.080
Jack/Lloyd offers Dan a drink of whiskey and
tells him to "take your medicine," a famous

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.980
line from the book that didn't survive in
Kubrick's screenplay.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:49.700
In addition, when the ghosts take over Dan's
body, Abra calls him a "false face," just

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.480
as Danny called his own father in the climax
of the novel version of The Shining.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.200
Some bits of continuity between the novel
and the film version of The Shining were too

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.060
tough to simply adjust outright.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:05.060
For starters, the Overlook Hotel doesn't even
really exist in the Doctor Sleep novel; instead,

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.540
Dan and Abra wander through the wreckage as
they battle against Rose the Hat and her cronies.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.540
That's because the Overlook is destroyed at
the end of The Shining novel as Jack realizes

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.260
what he's done and battles against his possession.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.340
Once again, Flanagan nods at that narrative
by having Dan basically do the same thing,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.520
starting the boiler in order to destroy the
Overlook.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.560
One detail from the Doctor Sleep novel that
didn't make it into the film actually has

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.719
nothing to do with Stanley Kubrick at all;
instead, it was clearly just too complicated

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.020
to work within a 150-minute runtime.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:38.639
In the film, Abra calls Dan "uncle," first
as a cover story for why he's being visited

00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:42.340
at work by a teenage girl, and later in the
film as a sign of affection.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.590
In the novel, he actually is her uncle, since
Abra's mother is his half-sister by way of

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:47.240
their father.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.740
Dan also learns all this from Abra's grandmother
who's dying of cancer, which all seems a bit

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.200
too complicated for an already-packed film.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.319
Meanwhile, some things are just left deliberately
ambiguous about whether they occured in Doctor

00:03:59.320 --> 00:03:59.960
Sleep's universe.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:04.340
For starters, Dick Hallorann appears in the
film, first to offer young Danny a trick to

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.180
keep the ghosts trapped in his mind, and then
later to push Dan to help Abra.

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:12.639
Dick never mentions how he died, or even if
he really died at all - considering the psychic

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.040
powers on display in Doctor Sleep, he could
just be paying Danny a visit via Shining.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:21.300
That ambiguity is a reference to the fact
that Hallorann's fate is pretty different

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:24.120
between the novel and the film version of
The Shining.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.810
In the novel, Hallorann unquestionably survives,
helping Danny and Wendy to mourn in Maine

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.680
after the trio escape the Overlook.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.860
In the film, he's killed by an axe-wielding
Jack just as he arrives to help Danny at the

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:35.360
Overlook.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:40.160
That specific axe shows up in Doctor Sleep
when Dan uses it to attack Rose, but there's

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.460
never any mention of it as the specific weapon
that killed Hallorann.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.820
Whether he's a ghost or not, his presence
does provide some Easter eggs.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:51.560
For one, when he meets Danny to talk about
ghosts in 1980, he sits in the same position

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:55.460
that Dan will later sit while discussing the
power with Abra in 2018.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:00.260
Since Dan ends the film as a ghostly visitor
to Abra in the same way that Dick first appears,

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.340
it seems he takes after his old mentor in
more ways than one.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.680
If you've seen any of Mike Flanagan's other
horror works, you'll probably recognize a

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.580
familiar face or two in Doctor Sleep.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.840
The writer-director loves to pack his film
and television projects with actors that he's

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:14.900
worked with before.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:19.620
Several members of the True Knot also appeared
in The Haunting of Hill House TV series, like

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.820
Catherine Parker, Robert Longstreet, and Selena
Anduze.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:27.840
Meanwhile, Bruce Greenwood and Carel Struycken,
who played Dr. John and Grandpa Flick in Doctor

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.340
Sleep, respectively, both appeared in Flanagan's
previous Stephen King adaptation, Gerald's

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:32.840
Game.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:37.340
They're not the only familiar faces on display
in Doctor Sleep, although you'd be forgiven

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:38.780
if you missed this little cameo.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.400
The original Danny Torrance, Danny Lloyd,
appears as an onlooker during a Little League

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.920
game midway through the film.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.440
His appearance is a blink-and-you'll-miss-it
cameo, but at least you won't need to ask

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.980
Tony if you missed something while watching
Doctor Sleep.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.940
Doctor Sleep isn't Mike Flanagan's first adaptation
of a Stephen King novel; first came 2017's

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:57.640
Gerald's Game.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.740
The story follows a woman named Jesse who,
after some bedroom play gone wrong with her

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.160
husband Gerald, finds herself handcuffed to
the bed with no one around for help.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:10.360
In the novel and the film, Jesse eventually
escapes from her handcuffs by slicing the

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.180
skin off her right hand in order to slide
out of her bonds.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.500
Flanagan certainly didn't forget his prior
King adaptation while directing Doctor Sleep,

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.080
since there's a small reference to Jesse's
ingenuity.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.260
In the film, Rose the Hat falls into a trap
while trying to invade Abra's mind.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:28.800
The file cabinet of Abra's memories slam shut
on Rose's hand as Abra toys with her.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.380
Like Jesse, Abra rips the skin off her right
hand in order to escape, although the success

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:35.680
leaves both women with a gnarly-looking wound.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:40.900
Stephen King is considered by many to be the
master of horror, but he's also a master at

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:41.400
franchising.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:45.900
Nearly all of his books, and plenty of his
short stories, all take place within the Dark

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:50.750
Tower universe, which encompasses everything
from It and Doctor Sleep to the actual Dark

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:51.600
Tower novels.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:57.940
"Inside this circle is your world, my world,
many others.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:06:59.500
Not that many."

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.860
Unsurprisingly, the film version of Doctor
Sleep is packed to the brim with references

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:05.800
to the series.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:10.270
When Dick visits Dan at the hospice, he tells
him "Ka is a wheel," a quote straight from

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:11.440
the Dark Tower novels.

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:17.220
Less obvious are references to two competing
forces in the Dark Tower universe: the Tet

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.620
corporation and LaMerk Industries.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:23.380
Tet shows up in Doctor Sleep as Tet Transit,
the bus system that brings Dan to Frazier

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:25.500
and, later in the film, Abra to Dan.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.930
In the Dark Tower, the Tet Corporation is
largely benevolent, so it makes sense that

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:33.620
it would appear in the film to take Dan and
Abra to where they can do the most good.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.940
Meanwhile, LaMerk Industries owns the park
where the True Knot torture and kill a young

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:38.740
baseball player.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.300
In the books, LaMerk is allied with the Crimson
King, who's one of the worst evils in King's

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:46.960
universe, so of course they'd own the perfect
lot in which to bury a body.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:51.450
Finally, the baseball player that the True
Knot kills wears the jersey number 19, which

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:53.860
is an integral number in the world of the
Dark Tower series.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.820
While saying his goodbyes to Abra, Dan tells
her to "shine on" because the world needs,

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.020
quote, "people who stand."

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:04.880
Dan's not wrong: the world in King's universe
really does need people who stand, considering

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:08.890
that there's a massive novel called The Stand
that involves a divine battle between good

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:09.400
and evil.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:14.580
The Stand has been adapted before: first as
a 1994 television miniseries, although it

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:17.720
hasn't reached the pantheon of some of King's
better-known adaptations.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:22.260
Dan's cryptic words might be a reference to
the new The Stand TV miniseries which will

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:25.780
premiere on CBS All Access, which is co-owned
by Warner Bros.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.820
It may turn out that the only thing needed
to defeat Randall Flagg and the Captain Trips

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.540
virus was corporate synergy.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.700
It's not all Stephen King Easter eggs in Doctor
Sleep.

00:08:34.969 --> 00:08:39.620
Mike Flanagan actually took the time to acknowledge
another modern-day horror maestro: director

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:40.200
Ti West.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:45.000
Like Flanagan, West started directing in the
2000s, achieving similar mainstream acclaim

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.180
for his work on films like The Innkeepers,
The Sacrament, and The House of the Devil.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:54.060
That last film starred Jocelin Donahue as
an unlucky babysitter in the 1980s who signs

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:58.100
up for a last-minute babysitting job that
goes very wrong, very quickly.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.800
Donahue also appears in Doctor Sleep as Abra's
mother, so it makes sense that Flanagan would

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.130
make a small reference to her House of the
Devil role.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.800
While Danny and Dick are chatting on the bench
in a flashback to 1980, a bulletin board can

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.560
be seen that's covered in flyers.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:16.480
Buried near the bottom and partially obscured
is a flyer advertising for a babysitting job.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.709
Granted, the flyer itself doesn't bear much
of a visual reference to the absurdly stark

00:09:20.709 --> 00:09:24.839
advertisement that was seen in House of the
Devil, but the timeline and Donahue's casting

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.620
in Doctor Sleep means that its inclusion probably
wasn't a coincidence.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.760
It's no stretch to say that Doctor Sleep is
intensely concerned with dreams and nightmares.

00:09:33.769 --> 00:09:38.439
The title literally references sleep, and
both Dan and Abra experience gruesome and

00:09:38.439 --> 00:09:39.660
upsetting nightmares.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:44.110
The big problem for those two is that their
nightmares tend to result in real-life consequences,

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:47.540
which might seem familiar to anyone who's
seen the Nightmare on Elm Street films.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:52.300
Wes Craven's famous horror franchise likewise
envisioned a monster that could invade your

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.400
dreams and affect your real-life body.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.000
"It's just a nightmare!"

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:57.580
"That's enough."

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.920
Flanagan paid homage to Craven's classic creation
with an Elm Street sign that's clearly visible

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:09.510
off of the main street in Frazier, the town
where Dan spends most of Doctor Sleep.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:13.180
Considering that Dan also spends a good chunk
of Doctor Sleep having trouble sleeping, we

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:17.120
think it's safe to say that there were definitely
nightmares on Elm Street in Doctor Sleep.

